-- Migration: KO-CURR-001 Curriculum v2.0 overhaul
-- Scope: 32-lesson free foundation-first track; integrate topics, sources, lessons, variants, and reviews.
-- Idempotency: Uses ON CONFLICT for upserts; deactivation filters on is_active = TRUE.

BEGIN;

-- 1. Insert new topics from curriculum-v2.md §1.3
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

-- 2. Insert new sources from sources-v2.csv (skipping existing OJK-001..008, BI-001..007, IDX-001..007, GLB-001..010)
ON CONFLICT (source_code) DO NOTHING;

-- 3. Deactivate low-quality variants (templated/generic/abstract content)
UPDATE content_variants cv
SET is_active = FALSE
WHERE cv.is_active = TRUE
  AND (
    -- Templated example pattern
    (
      cv.variant_type = 'example'
      AND cv.body->>'text' ILIKE 'Example:%'
      AND cv.body->>'text' ILIKE '%This illustrates how%'
    )
    -- Templated explanation pattern
    OR (
      cv.variant_type = 'explanation'
      AND cv.body->>'text' ILIKE '%is a fundamental concept in%'
      AND cv.body->>'text' ILIKE '%every financially literate person should understand%'
    )
    -- Garbage answers
    OR (
      cv.variant_type = 'question'
      AND cv.body->>'answer' = 'Check OJK license'
      AND cv.body->>'question' NOT ILIKE '%OJK%'
    )
    OR (
      cv.variant_type = 'question'
      AND cv.body->>'answer' = 'Possible loss of capital'
      AND cv.body->>'question' NOT ILIKE '%risk%'
    )
    OR (
      cv.variant_type = 'question'
      AND cv.body->>'answer' = 'A fundamental financial concept'
    )
    OR (
      cv.variant_type = 'question'
      AND cv.body->>'answer' = 'Everyone managing money'
    )
    -- Lottery distractor
    OR (
      cv.variant_type = 'question'
      AND cv.body->'options' @> '["Buying lottery tickets"]'::jsonb
    )
    -- Generic true/false explanations
    OR (
      cv.variant_type = 'question'
      AND cv.body->>'explanation' IN ('This statement is correct.', 'This statement is incorrect.')
    )
    -- Exact duplicate question text per lesson (keep lowest id)
    OR cv.id IN (
      SELECT id
      FROM (
        SELECT
          id,
          ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
            PARTITION BY lesson_id, body->>'question'
            ORDER BY id
          ) AS rn
        FROM content_variants
        WHERE variant_type = 'question'
          AND is_active = TRUE
      ) sub
      WHERE rn > 1
    )
  );

-- 4. Deactivate advanced draft lessons
UPDATE lessons
SET is_published = FALSE, review_status = 'draft', updated_at = NOW()
WHERE slug IN (
  'money_basics-advanced',
  'inflation-advanced',
  'budgeting-advanced',
  'risk_return-advanced',
  'idx_basics-advanced',
  'behavioral_finance-advanced'
);

-- 5 & 6. Upsert all 32 v2.0 lessons

Cash, GoPay balance, bank transfers, and QRIS payments are all forms of money because sellers accept them. Money only works because people trust it. That trust is backed by Bank Indonesia, which issues rupiah and keeps it stable. Without a shared form of money, trade would require barter, which is slow and inconvenient when the seller does not want what you have. Understanding these three roles makes later lessons about inflation, saving, and investing much easier to follow. Recognizing these three roles helps you see why keeping all your money as cash is not the only option, and why digital payments work just as well as paper notes in most Indonesian shops.', 'Bayu wants to buy a bowl of mie ayam at a warung for Rp 15,000. The seller does not want to trade for Bayu''s old earphones, so Bayu pays with a Rp 20,000 note and receives Rp 5,000 change. His friend Citra pays with GoPay because she has no cash. Both payments work because rupiah and GoPay balances are accepted as money in this warung.', 'If you understand what money actually is, you can decide where to keep it, when to spend it, and when to let it grow instead of leaving it idle.', 'Thinking money is only physical cash and ignoring digital wallets, bank balances, and other accepted payment forms.', 'Help the user explain the three roles of money using a simple daily transaction in Indonesia. Avoid investing topics; keep the discussion focused on exchange, saving, and price comparison.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'value-and-purchasing-power', 'Value and Purchasing Power', 'Nilai dan Daya Beli', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'value_purchasing_power'), 2, 'beginner', 50, 5, 'The same rupiah can buy different amounts depending on where and when you spend it.', 'Value means how useful or desirable something is to you. Purchasing power means how much you can actually buy with a fixed amount of money. The same Rp 50,000 buys more in some places and less in others because prices differ. A high nominal salary does not always mean a rich life if rent, food, and transport are expensive.

Purchasing power also changes over time. If prices rise, your money buys less. If prices fall, your money buys more. This matters when you compare incomes, prices, or savings across cities or years. Economists sometimes use the term real value to mean value adjusted for purchasing power. A high nominal amount does not always mean you are rich if everything around you is expensive. Understanding this helps you compare job offers, plan trips, and judge whether a price is fair. When you travel from your hometown to Jakarta, you may notice the same meal costs more. That is purchasing power in action. Comparing nominal amounts without checking what they can buy is like comparing two runners by their shoe sizes instead of their speed.', 'A bowl of mie ayam costs Rp 15,000 in Jakarta and Rp 12,000 in Yogyakarta. With Rp 50,000, Bayu can buy three bowls in Jakarta with Rp 5,000 left over, but four bowls in Yogyakarta with Rp 2,000 left over. The same Rp 50,000 has different purchasing power in each city.', 'When you plan a budget or compare salaries, purchasing power tells you the real story behind the rupiah number.', 'Believing that a bigger salary in a more expensive city always means a better life, without checking what that salary can actually buy.', 'Explain value and purchasing power with a price comparison from two Indonesian cities. Use food, transport, or pulsa examples familiar to an 18-year-old.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'inflation-101', 'Inflation: Why Your Rupiah Buys Less Over Time', 'Inflasi: Mengapa Rupiahmu Membeli Lebih Sedikit dari Waktu ke Waktu', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'inflation'), 3, 'beginner', 55, 6, 'Inflation is the slow rise in prices that quietly shrinks the buying power of your cash.', 'Inflation is a general increase in the prices of goods and services over time. When inflation happens, each rupiah buys less than before. Bank Indonesia aims to keep inflation low and stable, around 2.5% plus or minus 1% per year. Low inflation is normal in a growing economy, but high inflation hurts people who keep large amounts of cash.

Inflation does not mean every price rises by the same amount, but the overall cost of living tends to go up. Cash sitting under a pillow loses purchasing power because the same money buys fewer things next year. Savings accounts that pay interest can slow this loss, but only if the interest rate is higher than inflation. This is why thinking about inflation matters before you choose where to keep long-term savings. Protecting purchasing power is one of the main reasons people later consider investments, but only after they have built stable savings habits. Inflation is usually caused by a mix of rising demand, higher production costs, and changes in the money supply. You do not need to become an economist, but you should know that inflation is a normal feature of modern economies. The danger is ignoring it, not understanding it.', 'In 2020, Rina could buy a bowl of mie ayam for Rp 12,000. In 2025, the same bowl costs Rp 16,000. She kept Rp 120,000 under her pillow for five years. In 2020 that money bought ten bowls, but in 2025 it buys only seven bowls and leaves some change. Her rupiah amount stayed the same, but her purchasing power fell.', 'Inflation affects every saving and spending choice. Knowing it exists helps you protect your money instead of watching it quietly shrink.', 'Keeping all long-term savings as cash and assuming the face value of the money will stay the same forever.', 'Use a mie ayam price example to show how purchasing power falls over time. Mention Bank Indonesia''s inflation target cautiously; do not give investment advice.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'income-vs-wealth', 'Income vs Wealth', 'Pendapatan vs Kekayaan', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'income_wealth'), 4, 'beginner', 50, 5, 'Income is money coming in; wealth is what you keep and grow over time.', 'Income is the flow of money you receive, such as salary, allowance, or freelance payments. Wealth is the stock of money and valuable things you already own, minus what you owe. A person can earn a high income but have low wealth if they spend almost everything. Another person can earn less but have more wealth if they save and invest consistently.

Income measures speed; wealth measures the size of your financial reservoir. Long-term financial health depends more on what you keep than on what you earn. This is why budgeting, controlling debt, and forming saving habits matter at every income level. You can think of income as water flowing into a bucket and wealth as the water already inside. If the bucket has holes, a fast flow will not fill it. Fixing the holes, saving more, and avoiding high-interest debt are what build wealth over time. Wealth also includes things that can be sold, such as a motorbike or valuable skills, minus any debts attached to them. This is why two people with the same salary can end up in very different financial positions. The gap comes from choices, not just earnings.', 'Andi works as a sales promoter and earns Rp 8,000,000 per month. He rents an expensive apartment, eats out daily, and has zero savings. Budi works as a barista and earns Rp 4,000,000 per month. He lives simply and has saved Rp 15,000,000 in a separate tabungan. Budi has less income but more wealth because he keeps more of what he earns.', 'At 18, your income may be small, but your wealth-building habits can start now. Wealth is built by what you do not spend.', 'Judging financial success only by monthly income while ignoring savings, debt, and spending habits.', 'Contrast two fictional Indonesian earners: one with high income and low savings, one with lower income and higher savings. Help the user explain why wealth is about what is kept, not just what is earned.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'needs-vs-wants-101', 'Needs vs Wants', 'Kebutuhan vs Keinginan', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'money_basics'), 5, 'beginner', 50, 5, 'Covering needs first makes wants more enjoyable and less stressful.', 'A need is something you must have to live, study, or work safely. Examples include food, shelter, transport, and basic phone credit. A want is something you would like to have but can live without, such as a new outfit, a concert ticket, or a premium streaming subscription.

The line between need and want is not always clear. For example, a basic phone is a need for online classes, but the latest flagship phone is a want. The goal is not to eliminate wants. The goal is to cover needs first, save something next, and then spend on wants without guilt or debt. Many people feel stressed because they buy wants first and then struggle to pay for needs. Flipping the order creates breathing room and makes spending more enjoyable because you know the important things are already covered. Culture and social media often blur this line by turning wants into perceived needs. A basic phone becomes a need when classes move online, but a new color or brand is still a want. Learning to separate the two gives you control over your money instead of letting advertisers decide for you.', 'Sari gets Rp 1,500,000 monthly allowance. She pays kos Rp 700,000, buys a campus meal card for Rp 400,000, and tops up pulsa for Rp 100,000. Those are needs. She also wants a new pair of sneakers for Rp 600,000 and a streaming subscription for Rp 100,000. If she buys the sneakers first, she may not have enough for kos. If she covers needs and saves Rp 100,000 first, she can plan for the sneakers next month.', 'Learning this split early helps you avoid running out of money before the month ends and prevents impulse debt.', 'Calling every desire a need, then wondering why there is no money left for saving or emergencies.', 'Walk the user through a typical Indonesian student budget. Ask them to label each expense as need or want, and discuss why the order of spending matters.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'assets-vs-liabilities', 'Assets vs Liabilities', 'Aset vs Liabilitas', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'assets_liabilities'), 6, 'beginner', 55, 6, 'An asset puts money in your pocket; a liability takes money out.', 'An asset is something you own that can put money in your pocket or grow in value over time. A liability is something you owe or something that costs you money regularly. The same physical item can be an asset for one person and a liability for another, depending on how it is used.

For example, a motorbike bought with cash and used to earn income as an ojek online driver is an asset because it helps generate money. The same motorbike bought on credit just for weekend fun is a liability because it requires loan payments, fuel, and maintenance without producing income. Even a house can be an asset if it generates rental income, or a liability if the KPR bank loan and maintenance drain your cash. The key question is: does this item increase my future money or decrease it? Education and skills can also be assets because they may increase your future income. However, they only become assets if they lead to earning power. A course or degree that does not improve your job prospects can become a liability if it leaves you with heavy education debt.', 'Dodi buys a motorbike on credit for Rp 15,000,000 to become an ojek online driver. Every month he earns Rp 2,500,000 extra income and pays a Rp 800,000 installment. For him, the motorbike is an asset because it helps him earn more than it costs. His friend Eko buys the same motorbike on credit only for weekend rides to the mall. Eko pays the Rp 800,000 installment, fuel, and parking but earns nothing from it. For Eko, the motorbike is a liability.', 'Before you buy something big, ask whether it will grow your money or drain it. This habit protects you from debt disguised as status.', 'Thinking that expensive possessions like cars or motorbikes are automatically assets, even when they mostly cost money.', 'Use the motorbike example to explain why the same item can be an asset or liability based on use. Help the user apply this test to their own spending decisions.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'understanding-risk', 'Understanding Risk', 'Memahami Risiko', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'risk_basics'), 7, 'beginner', 55, 6, 'Risk is the chance that reality turns out worse than you expected.', 'Risk is the possibility that an outcome will be worse than you hoped. It does not mean danger every time; it means uncertainty about results. Every financial decision carries some risk, even keeping cash under a pillow, because inflation can reduce its value.

Risk becomes easier to manage when you name it clearly. Ask yourself: what could go wrong? How likely is it? How bad would it be? Can I reduce the chance or limit the damage? Answering these questions before acting is the foundation of good financial behavior. For example, carrying an umbrella reduces the risk of getting wet, but it does not remove the chance of rain. In finance, diversification, emergency funds, and insurance are tools that reduce specific risks without eliminating all uncertainty. You can think of risk as having two parts: the chance that something bad happens, and the size of the loss if it does. Some risks are worth taking, like investing in your education. Others are not, like lending money to a stranger online. The skill is telling the difference.', 'Rina lends Rp 200,000 to a classmate who often pays back late. The reward is helping a friend and possibly being helped later. The risk is that the friend never pays back on time, leaving Rina short on pulsa money. Before lending, Rina can reduce the risk by only lending what she can afford to lose or by agreeing on a clear payback date.', 'If you can spot risk early, you can protect your savings, avoid scams, and make calmer choices under pressure.', 'Thinking that avoiding all decisions removes risk. Doing nothing with money also carries risk, especially from inflation.', 'Use a non-investing example like lending money to a friend. Help the user identify the possible downside, the likelihood, and a simple way to reduce the risk.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'time-value-of-money', 'Time Value of Money', 'Nilai Waktu Uang', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'time_value_money'), 8, 'beginner', 60, 6, 'Money available sooner is usually worth more than the same amount later.', 'The time value of money means that receiving the same amount of money earlier is generally better than receiving it later. The reason is opportunity cost: money in your hand today can cover needs, earn interest, or be saved for a goal. Money promised in the future may never arrive, and prices may rise while you wait.

This idea also works in reverse. If you borrow Rp 1,000,000 today, you usually must repay more later because the lender gives up the use of that money. Time matters because options and certainty matter. Starting to save early gives compounding more time to work, which is one reason small savings started at 18 can grow larger than bigger savings started at 35. Understanding this now prepares you for lessons on interest, saving, and investing. This concept also explains why installment payments often cost more than paying upfront. The seller is giving up the use of their money, so they charge extra for the delay. Whenever you delay a payment or a receipt, time value of money is part of the deal.', 'Bayu is owed Rp 500,000 by a relative. He can be paid today or in three months. If he receives it today, he can buy a phone charger he needs for online classes and still have money left for transport. If he waits three months, the charger may cost more, or he may miss classes because his phone is dead. Receiving the money today gives him more options.', 'Time affects the value of your money. Starting to save early, paying debts on time, and choosing when to receive payments all become clearer with this mental model.', 'Waiting to save or invest until you have a large amount, not realizing that earlier small amounts often grow more than later large amounts.', 'Explain time value of money with a simple choice: receive money now or later. Discuss what the user could do with the money today and what might change while waiting.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'budgeting-101', 'Budgeting: The 50/30/20 Rule, Indonesian Style', 'Anggaran: Aturan 50/30/20 ala Indonesia', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'budgeting'), 9, 'beginner', 60, 6, 'A budget is a plan that splits your income into needs, wants, and savings or debt payments.', 'A budget is a written plan for how you will spend and save your money. The 50/30/20 framework is one simple starting point: about 50% for needs, 30% for wants, and 20% for savings and paying off debt. In Indonesia, you may need to adjust these numbers based on family contributions, living costs, and local prices. A student living with parents might save 30%, while someone paying full kos might only save 10%.

The real value of a budget is not perfection. It is awareness. When you know where your money is going, you can spot leaks, plan for goals, and avoid panic at the end of the month. A budget only works if you compare the plan with what you actually spend and adjust as life changes. Think of it as a map, not a prison. Many Indonesian students receive irregular allowances or freelance income, so a strict 50/30/20 split may not fit every month. The important thing is to have categories and limits, then adjust as your income changes. A flexible budget beats a perfect budget that you abandon after one week.', 'Ani earns Rp 2,000,000 per month from a part-time job. She spends Rp 1,000,000 on needs like kos, campus meals, and pulsa. She allocates Rp 600,000 for wants like streaming subscriptions and hanging out with friends. That leaves Rp 400,000 for savings and paying back a small debt to her brother. This simple split keeps her from borrowing more and lets her build an emergency fund over time.', 'Budgeting turns money stress into a clear plan. It is the single best tool for stretching a small income without feeling deprived.', 'Creating a perfect budget on paper but never tracking actual spending, so the plan becomes useless.', 'Guide the user to build a 50/30/20 budget using Indonesian rupiah and student expenses. Emphasize tracking actual spending, not just planning.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'emergency-fund-101', 'Emergency Fund: Prepare Before You Invest', 'Dana Darurat: Siapkan Sebelum Investasi', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'emergency_fund'), 10, 'beginner', 65, 6, 'An emergency fund is a separate safety net that protects your long-term savings from surprise expenses.', 'An emergency fund is money set aside specifically for urgent, unexpected costs such as illness, job loss, or a broken phone. A common guide is to save three to six months of regular expenses. If your income is stable and you have no dependents, three months may be enough. If your income is irregular or you support family, aim closer to six months or more.

The fund should be kept in a safe and easy-to-access place, such as a separate savings account at an LPS-insured bank, a short-term deposit, or a low-risk money market fund. It should not be in stocks, crypto, or anything whose value can drop sharply when you suddenly need the money. The purpose is stability, not high returns. Building this fund before investing protects your long-term plans from short-term shocks. Start small if you must. Even one month of expenses is better than nothing. The goal is to build the habit of separating emergency money from spending money. Once the fund is in place, you will feel less anxiety when unexpected costs appear, and you can think more clearly about investing.', 'Budi is an ojek online driver with irregular income. His monthly expenses for kos, food, and fuel total Rp 3,000,000. One day his phone breaks, costing Rp 3,000,000 to repair. Because he has kept Rp 18,000,000 in a separate tabungan as an emergency fund, he can fix the phone without borrowing from a pinjol app or selling his motorbike.', 'An emergency fund protects your future plans. Without it, a small crisis can force you into high-interest debt or make you sell investments at a loss.', 'Mixing emergency money with daily spending, or chasing higher returns by putting emergency savings into risky assets.', 'Help the user calculate a simple emergency fund target based on monthly expenses. Recommend safe, liquid places to store it and warn against risky investments for this money.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'pay-yourself-first', 'Pay Yourself First', 'Bayar Dirimu Sendiri Dulu', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'saving_habits'), 11, 'beginner', 55, 5, 'Save automatically at the start of the month instead of hoping something is left at the end.', 'Pay yourself first means transferring money to savings before you spend on anything else. Instead of waiting to see what is left at the end of the month, you decide in advance how much to save and move it immediately when income arrives.

This works because willpower is limited. If saving is the last priority, it often disappears into small daily purchases like extra coffee, snacks, or impulse app purchases. Automating the transfer makes saving a default, not a decision. Even a small amount saved first builds a habit that becomes easier as income grows. Over time, paying yourself first becomes invisible, and your savings grow without requiring constant discipline. You can start with just Rp 50,000 or Rp 100,000 per month. The amount matters less than the habit. Once the system is in place, you can increase it whenever your income rises. The magic is that saving happens without requiring a daily decision.', 'Every month when Doni''s Rp 2,000,000 part-time salary arrives, his banking app automatically moves Rp 300,000 to a separate tabungan labeled ''Future Me.'' Only then does he buy pulsa, coffee, or hang out with friends. Some months he has less to spend, but his savings grow steadily because they leave his account first.', 'This habit turns saving from a struggle into a routine. It is one of the most reliable ways to build wealth even on a small income.', 'Planning to save whatever is left after spending, then being surprised when nothing remains.', 'Suggest practical ways to automate savings, such as scheduled bank transfers or separate tabungan buckets. Use a part-time worker example in Indonesia.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'spending-traps', 'Spending Traps', 'Jebakan Pengeluaran', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'spending_behavior'), 12, 'beginner', 55, 5, 'Flash sales, influencer hype, and small daily treats can quietly drain your budget.', 'A spending trap is anything that tricks you into buying more than you planned. Common traps include flash sales that create fake urgency, influencer recommendations that make you feel left out, and small daily treats that seem cheap alone but add up fast. Sellers use countdown timers, limited-stock labels, and social proof to push you toward quick decisions.

The best defense is a pause. Before buying, ask: did I plan for this yesterday? Can I afford it from my wants budget? Will I still want this in one week? Giving yourself even one day to decide often breaks the spell of impulse spending. This does not mean never enjoying life. It means choosing your wants deliberately so your money goes to things you actually value. Another trap is bundling, where you buy more to get free shipping or a small discount. A third is lifestyle inflation, where spending rises as soon as income rises. Defending against these traps does not mean living like a monk. It means spending on purpose, not by accident.', 'Dina opens Shopee and sees a flash sale: ''buy 2 get 1 free'' on a skincare set priced at Rp 450,000 that she does not need. The countdown timer says two hours left. She feels pressure to buy now. Before checking out, she checks her wants budget and realizes she already spent Rp 350,000 on a concert ticket. She closes the app and saves the skincare for next month if she still wants it.', 'At 18, your money is small but your habits are forming. Learning to resist traps now protects much larger incomes later.', 'Buying something just because it is on sale, even though you would not have bought it at full price.', 'Discuss flash sales, influencer hype, and small daily treats using Indonesian e-commerce and social media contexts. Teach the user the ''pause and check the budget'' defense.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'debt-traps', 'Debt Traps: PayLater, Pinjol, and Credit Cards', 'Jebakan Utang: PayLater, Pinjol, dan Kartu Kredit', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'debt_management'), 13, 'beginner', 60, 6, 'High-interest consumer debt can grow faster than your ability to pay it back.', 'Debt is money you borrow and promise to repay later, usually with extra cost called interest. Debt becomes a trap when the interest or late fees grow faster than your income. In Indonesia, PayLater apps, online loans (pinjol), and credit cards make borrowing feel easy, but missing a payment can cause the balance to balloon.

The safest strategy is to avoid borrowing for things that lose value quickly, such as clothes or gadgets. If you already have high-interest debt, the fastest escape is usually to pay the highest-interest debt first while making minimum payments on others. This is called the avalanche method. The goal is to stop the debt from growing before it controls your monthly budget. Paying only the minimum is usually the slowest and most expensive path. Minimum payments are designed to keep you in debt longer because the interest keeps growing. If you only pay the minimum on a high-interest loan, you may end up paying more in interest than the original amount you borrowed. That is why stopping the growth is the first priority.', 'Raka uses PayLater to buy a Rp 1,200,000 hoodie. He forgets the due date and the bill grows to Rp 1,500,000 after late fees and interest. His monthly allowance is only Rp 1,500,000, so now most of it is gone before he buys food or pulsa. He should have saved for the hoodie first or skipped it, and now he must negotiate a payment plan and stop using PayLater until it is paid off.', 'One small debt can swallow your entire monthly budget. Understanding how debt grows helps you say no to borrowing for wants.', 'Using PayLater or a credit card for wants without reading the interest rate and due date.', 'Explain how PayLater, pinjol, and credit card interest can cause debt to grow. Walk through an escape plan focused on paying highest-interest debt first.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'goal-setting-101', 'Financial Goal Setting', 'Menetapkan Tujuan Keuangan', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'goal_setting'), 14, 'beginner', 55, 5, 'Clear goals with amounts and deadlines turn wishes into achievable plans.', 'A financial goal is a target you set for your money. Good goals are specific, have an amount, and have a deadline. Vague goals like ''save more'' rarely work because there is no way to measure progress. A better goal is ''save Rp 5,000,000 for a laptop by December.''

Goals are usually grouped by time. Short-term goals are under one year, such as buying a phone. Medium-term goals are one to five years, such as building an emergency fund or funding a course. Long-term goals are more than five years, such as starting a business or building a pension fund (dana pensiun). Breaking a big goal into monthly savings makes it feel possible. Writing the goal down and tracking progress each month dramatically increases the chance of success. Sharing your goal with a trusted friend or family member can increase accountability. You can also use visual reminders, such as a savings tracker on your phone. The more concrete the goal, the easier it is to resist small temptations that would delay it.', 'Fani wants a Rp 5,000,000 laptop in 10 months for college. Instead of hoping money appears, she divides the target by ten. She needs to save Rp 500,000 per month. She sets an automatic transfer of Rp 500,000 to a separate tabungan on payday. Each month she checks her progress and adjusts spending if she falls behind.', 'Clear goals give your budget a purpose. They make saying no to impulse spending easier because you know what you are saying yes to instead.', 'Setting vague goals like ''save more'' without a number or deadline, then feeling stuck when nothing changes.', 'Help the user turn a vague wish into a SMART-style goal with a rupiah amount and deadline. Break it into monthly savings and suggest how to track progress.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'too-good-to-be-true', 'Too Good To Be True', 'Terlalu Bagus untuk Jadi Kenyataan', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'scam_defense'), 15, 'beginner', 60, 5, 'Guaranteed high returns with no risk do not exist; that promise is a classic scam sign.', 'If an investment promises high returns, zero risk, and pressure to recruit friends, it is almost certainly a scam. In finance, return and risk are connected. Anyone promising guaranteed profit is either lying or hiding the risk. OJK regularly warns the public about illegal investments that use these exact tricks.

Three red flags to remember are guaranteed returns, no risk, and recruitment bonuses. Real investing involves uncertainty, and even regulated products can lose value. The safest response is to stop, check the OJK license, and ignore the fear of missing out. No legitimate opportunity disappears in two hours. If you feel rushed, that is a signal to walk away. Scammers often use fake testimonials, luxury photos, and urgent deadlines to overwhelm your judgment. They target young people who are new to investing and eager to grow their money quickly. Remember that building wealth is usually slow; anything that promises speed deserves extra suspicion.', 'Bayu is added to a Telegram group that promises ''guaranteed 20% profit per month, zero risk, invite your friends and earn more.'' The group asks members to deposit at least Rp 1,000,000. His friend pressures him to join before the ''slots close.'' Bayu remembers that OJK says guaranteed returns are impossible. He checks the OJK Waspada Investasi portal, does not find the product, and leaves the group.', 'One scam can wipe out your savings and trust in money. Spotting red flags early protects you and your friends.', 'Joining an investment because friends are joining, without checking if the product is licensed by OJK.', 'List the three red flags of investment fraud: guaranteed returns, no risk, and recruitment pressure. Use a Telegram or WhatsApp group example and direct the user to OJK''s Waspada Investasi portal.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'check-ojk-license', 'Check Before You Invest: OJK License', 'Cek Sebelum Investasi: Izin OJK', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ojk_license_check'), 16, 'beginner', 60, 6, 'Always verify that a financial product is licensed by OJK before sending any money.', 'OJK, the Financial Services Authority of Indonesia, licenses and supervises banks, insurance companies, investment managers, and many investment products. A product that is not listed on OJK''s website is illegal and unprotected. If the company fails or runs away with your money, you have little chance of recovery.

Before investing, visit the official OJK website and use the Waspada Investasi or licensing search tools. Check the company name, product name, and license number. If a salesperson is legitimate, they will not pressure you to skip this step. Taking five minutes to verify can save you from losing everything. The habit of checking before sending money is the most important protection an 18-year-old investor can build. Do not trust screenshots or links sent by salespeople, because these can be faked. Always type the official OJK website address yourself or use a bookmark. If the product is legitimate, the license check will be quick. If it is not, you will have saved yourself from a scam.', 'A friend tells Bayu about a ''crypto staking'' app that pays 15% per month and asks him to deposit Rp 500,000 to start. The friend shows screenshots of profits. Before depositing, Bayu opens the official OJK website, searches the company name, and finds nothing. He also searches the OJK Waspada Investasi list and sees similar apps flagged. He tells his friend that the app is unlicensed and dangerous.', 'Checking a license takes minutes but prevents losses that could take years to recover from. It is the most important investing habit you can build.', 'Trusting a friend, influencer, or screenshot without verifying the product on OJK''s official website.', 'Describe the exact steps to check an OJK license: go to ojk.go.id, find the Waspada Investasi or licensing search, enter the company or product name, and confirm it appears. Use a crypto or investment app example.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'phishing-social-engineering', 'Phishing and Social Engineering', 'Phishing dan Rekayasa Sosial', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'phishing_social_engineering'), 17, 'beginner', 55, 5, 'Scammers pretend to be trusted people or institutions to steal your information and money.', 'Phishing is a fake message designed to steal your personal data, passwords, or OTP. Social engineering is when a scammer manipulates you emotionally, for example by pretending to be a friend, family member, bank officer, or government official. These attacks often create urgency: ''your account will be blocked'' or ''your package is stuck.''

The safest response is to slow down. Do not click links in unexpected messages. Do not share OTPs, PINs, or passwords. If the message claims to be from your bank, open the official banking app or call the official number instead of replying to the message. Real banks never ask for your OTP. When in doubt, stop and verify through a trusted channel. Scammers also impersonate delivery companies, online shops, and even romantic interests. They often ask for small favors first to build trust. The moment someone asks for your PIN, OTP, or password, the conversation should end. No trusted institution will ever ask for these.', 'Bayu receives a WhatsApp message that says ''Your BCA account with a balance of Rp 5,000,000 is blocked. Click this link to reactivate.'' The link asks for his ATM card number and OTP. Bayu closes the message, opens his official BCA mobile app directly, and sees his account is fine. He then reports the number to his bank and blocks the sender.', 'One stolen OTP can empty your account in seconds. Learning to spot phishing protects your money, identity, and peace of mind.', 'Clicking links in SMS or WhatsApp messages without checking the sender, especially when the message creates panic.', 'Explain phishing and social engineering with a WhatsApp or SMS example. Emphasize that real banks never ask for OTPs and that users should open official apps directly instead of clicking links.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'mlm-pyramid-red-flags', 'MLM and Pyramid Red Flags', 'Tanda MLM dan Piramida', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'mlm_pyramid'), 18, 'beginner', 60, 6, 'A real business earns money from customers; a pyramid scheme earns money from recruits.', 'A legitimate multi-level marketing company mainly earns money by selling real products to real customers. A pyramid scheme mainly earns money by recruiting new members who pay fees or buy inventory. Pyramid schemes collapse when recruitment slows because there are not enough new people to pay the old ones.

Red flags include being paid mostly for recruiting, being forced to buy large stock packages, promises of easy wealth, and pressure to recruit friends and family quickly. If the income depends more on bringing people in than on selling products, it is likely a pyramid scheme. OJK warns the public to be careful with such offers. Remember, a real business can survive even if no new members join. Another warning sign is when the company discourages you from researching independently or tells you that skeptics are just negative. Legitimate businesses welcome questions and have clear product prices. If the business model depends on your network of friends, it deserves careful scrutiny.', 'Sari is invited to a skincare ''business.'' To join, she must buy Rp 3,000,000 worth of stock and recruit two friends. Her income mostly comes from the recruitment fees of the people she brings in, not from selling skincare. The products are overpriced and hard to sell. This is a pyramid scheme because it needs endless recruits to survive.', 'Pyramid schemes ruin friendships and finances. Knowing the difference between selling products and recruiting protects you from being used.', 'Joining a ''business opportunity'' because a friend or family member asks, without checking whether income comes from sales or recruitment.', 'Distinguish product-based MLM from recruitment-driven pyramid schemes. Use a skincare or supplement example and ask the user to identify whether income comes from customers or recruits.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'interest-101', 'What Is Interest?', 'Apa Itu Bunga?', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'interest'), 19, 'intermediate', 60, 5, 'Interest is the cost of borrowing money or the reward for saving it.', 'Interest is the extra money you pay when you borrow, or the extra money you receive when you save or lend. When you deposit money in a bank, the bank pays you interest because it can use your money to make loans. When you borrow, you pay interest because the lender gave up the use of that money.

Simple interest is calculated only on the original amount. For example, 4% annual interest on Rp 1,000,000 gives you Rp 40,000 in one year. The interest rate matters because it affects how fast debt grows and how fast savings grow. Always check whether the rate is per year, per month, or per day before comparing offers. A monthly rate that looks small can become very large when converted to an annual rate. Compound interest on debt is especially dangerous because the amount you owe can grow even if you stop borrowing. This is why high-interest loans can spiral out of control. Understanding interest from both sides, as a saver and as a borrower, helps you use it wisely and avoid traps.', 'Rina saves Rp 1,000,000 in a bank deposito that pays 4% per year. After one year, she earns Rp 40,000 in interest before tax. The bank uses her deposit and others like it to fund loans for businesses and other customers. Rina''s reward is the interest, and the bank''s reward is the difference between what it pays savers and what it charges borrowers.', 'Interest affects both sides of your money: what you earn on savings and what you pay on debt. Understanding it helps you compare products honestly.', 'Ignoring whether an interest rate is annual or monthly, which makes loans look cheaper than they really are.', 'Explain simple interest with a deposito or savings example. Show how to calculate annual interest on a small rupiah amount and warn about monthly vs annual rate confusion.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'compound-interest-101', 'Compound Interest: Money Making Money', 'Bunga Majemuk: Uang yang Membuat Uang', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'compound_interest'), 20, 'intermediate', 65, 6, 'Compound interest means earning interest on your interest, which grows faster over time.', 'Compound interest happens when you earn interest not only on the money you originally saved, but also on the interest that money has already earned. This creates a snowball effect: the longer you leave the money, the faster it grows. The two most important factors are time and the interest rate.

Starting early matters more than starting big. A small amount saved early can grow larger than a bigger amount saved later because it has more time to compound. Of course, in real life returns are not guaranteed, and the value can also fall. The lesson is about the power of time, not a promise of profit. Still, the math of compounding is one reason financial educators encourage young people to start saving as soon as possible. The rule of 72 is a simple way to estimate doubling time: divide 72 by your annual return rate. At 6% per year, money roughly doubles in 12 years. This is only a rough estimate and not a guarantee, but it shows why time is so powerful when combined with compounding.', 'Doni saves Rp 100,000 every month starting at age 18 in an instrument that averages 6% per year. His brother starts saving the same amount at age 28. By age 40, Doni has contributed only Rp 12,000,000 more than his brother but ends up with far more because his money compounded for ten extra years. The extra decade of compounding makes the difference.', 'At 18, time is your biggest advantage. Starting small now can beat starting big later because of compounding.', 'Waiting until you earn a large salary to start saving, not realizing that lost time cannot be bought back.', 'Illustrate compound interest with a comparison of two savers starting at different ages. Emphasize that this is a math concept, not a guaranteed return promise.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'bank-vs-investment', 'Bank vs Investment: Where Should Your Money Go?', 'Bank vs Investasi: Uangmu Harus ke Mana?', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'bank_vs_investment'), 21, 'intermediate', 60, 5, 'Banks protect short-term money; investments may grow long-term money but carry risk.', 'A bank account is a place to keep money safe and easy to access. Savings and deposits usually pay lower, more stable interest and are protected by programs like LPS within limits. Investments, such as stocks, bonds, or reksa dana, offer the chance of higher returns but also carry the risk of losing value.

The right place depends on when you need the money. Money you need soon, such as next month''s tuition or an emergency fund, belongs in a bank. Money you will not need for many years can be considered for investments, because you have time to recover from short-term drops. Never mix the two goals. Putting emergency money in stocks can force you to sell during a loss, while keeping long-term money such as pension savings in a low-interest account can let inflation eat it away. Liquidity means how quickly you can turn an asset into cash without losing much value. Bank savings are highly liquid. Stocks and reksa dana are less liquid because prices change daily and selling during a panic can mean losses. Match liquidity to your goal so you are never forced to sell at the wrong time.', 'Bayu has Rp 5,000,000. He needs Rp 2,000,000 next month for tuition, so he keeps it in a regular tabungan for safety. He can lock away Rp 3,000,000 for at least two years, so he considers a low-risk reksa dana. He does not put next month''s tuition in stocks because the value could drop right when he needs it.', 'Choosing the wrong place for your money can mean missing growth or being forced to sell at a loss. Match the tool to the goal.', 'Putting money you need soon into risky investments, hoping for quick gains before a known deadline.', 'Compare bank savings and investments by safety, liquidity, return, and time horizon. Use a split example: short-term money in a bank, long-term money in an investment.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'risk-return-101', 'Risk and Return', 'Risiko dan Pengembalian', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'risk_return'), 22, 'intermediate', 65, 6, 'Higher potential returns usually come with higher risk; there is no safe way to earn big quickly.', 'Return is the money you gain or lose on an investment over time. Risk is the chance that the actual return will be worse than expected. In general, investments that offer higher potential returns also carry higher risk. This is sometimes called the risk-return trade-off.

There is no free lunch in finance. If someone promises high return with little or no risk, they are likely hiding something or running a scam. Safe tools like bank deposits protect your money but usually grow slowly. Riskier tools like stocks or equity reksa dana can grow faster but can also fall sharply. Your job is to match the level of risk to your goal and your ability to handle losses without panicking. Your risk tolerance is personal. It depends on your income stability, your goals, and how you react to losses. Two people with the same age can have very different risk profiles. There is no single correct answer, but there is a correct process: match risk to the goal and to your own comfort level.', 'Rina has Rp 10,000,000 to set aside. A bank tabungan pays 2% per year with low risk; her balance is safe and easy to withdraw. A reksa dana saham has the potential for higher returns but its value can drop 10% or more in a bad month. If she needs the money in five months, the tabungan is better. If she can leave it for five years, the reksa dana saham may be worth considering because she has time to wait out short-term drops.', 'Understanding risk and return helps you avoid scams and choose tools that fit your goals without panic.', 'Chasing the highest return without asking how much money you could lose and how soon you need the money.', 'Explain the risk-return trade-off using a bank tabungan and a reksa dana saham. Ask the user about their time horizon before suggesting which tool might fit.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'diversification-101', 'Diversification: Don''t Put All Eggs in One Basket', 'Diversifikasi: Jangan Taruh Semua Telur dalam Satu Keranjang', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'diversification'), 23, 'intermediate', 60, 5, 'Spreading your money across different assets reduces the damage when one of them falls.', 'Diversification means spreading money across different investments, industries, or asset types so that one bad event does not wipe everything out. If all your money is in one stock and that company has a scandal, you could lose a lot. If your money is spread across many stocks, bonds, and cash, one problem has less impact.

Diversification does not guarantee profit or remove all risk. Markets can still fall broadly. But it reduces the risk that a single mistake or bad news event will destroy your savings. Later lessons will show how reksa dana can be an easy way to diversify for beginners. The idea is simple: do not let one bad day decide your entire financial future. Correlation means how closely two investments move together. If you own many bank stocks, they may all fall at the same time during a banking crisis. True diversification means owning things that do not all react the same way to the same news. That is why mixing stocks, bonds, and cash can help.', 'Warung A only sells mie ayam for Rp 15,000. When chicken prices spike, sales collapse because customers cannot afford the new price of Rp 20,000. Warung B sells mie ayam for Rp 15,000, es teh for Rp 5,000, and nasi goreng for Rp 18,000. If one item becomes expensive, customers can still buy the others. Warung B is more diversified because it is not dependent on one product.', 'Diversification protects you from overconfidence in one company, one trend, or one influencer stock tip.', 'Putting all your savings into one stock or crypto because a friend or TikTok creator said it will go up.', 'Use the warung example to explain diversification. Then connect it to investing: spreading money across different companies, sectors, or asset types to reduce single-event risk.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'reksa-dana-basics', 'Reksa Dana Basics', 'Dasar-Dasar Reksa Dana', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'reksa_dana'), 24, 'intermediate', 65, 6, 'A reksa dana pools money from many people and invests it through a licensed manager.', 'A reksa dana, or mutual fund, is a pool of money collected from many investors and managed by a licensed investment manager. When you buy units of a reksa dana, you own a small share of the whole pool. The fund invests in a mix of assets according to its rules.

The three main types are money market funds, which are the safest and most liquid; fixed income funds, which invest in bonds and offer moderate risk; and equity funds, which invest in stocks and carry higher risk with higher potential return. Reksa dana can be a simple way to diversify because your money is spread across many assets even if you only invest a small amount. Fees and past performance should be checked, but they do not guarantee future results. Each unit of a reksa dana has a net asset value, or NAV, which reflects the total value of the fund''s investments divided by the number of units. The NAV changes as the underlying investments change. When you buy or sell, you are buying or selling units at the current NAV, not directly owning the stocks or bonds inside.', 'Ani wants to start investing but does not know how to pick individual stocks. She opens an account with an OJK-registered investment manager and buys Rp 500,000 of an equity reksa dana. Her money is pooled with thousands of other investors and spread across many companies. She owns a tiny slice of each company in the fund, giving her instant diversification without choosing stocks herself.', 'Reksa dana lets beginners start small, diversify, and rely on licensed managers instead of guessing which stock to buy.', 'Choosing a reksa dana based only on last year''s high return, without checking the fund type and risk level.', 'Explain reksa dana as a pooled investment managed by an OJK-registered investment manager. Describe money market, fixed income, and equity funds in order of risk.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'what-is-a-stock', 'What Is a Stock?', 'Apa Itu Saham?', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'stocks'), 25, 'intermediate', 60, 5, 'A stock is a small piece of ownership in a company.', 'A stock, or saham, is a share of ownership in a company. When you buy a stock, you become one of the owners of that company, even if your share is tiny. As an owner, you may benefit if the company grows and becomes more valuable, but you may also lose money if the company performs poorly.

Stock prices change because buyers and sellers disagree on what a company is worth. If more people want to buy than sell, the price tends to rise. If more people want to sell than buy, the price tends to fall. Owning stock is not gambling, but it is not a guaranteed way to make money either. It is ownership in a real business, and the value of that business changes as the world changes. Companies may also pay dividends, which are portions of profit distributed to shareholders. Not all companies pay dividends; some reinvest profits to grow faster. As a shareholder, you have a claim on the company''s assets and earnings proportional to the shares you own, though this does not mean you can walk into the office and give orders.', 'Bayu buys one lot of BBCA, which is Bank Central Asia stock listed on IDX, at Rp 8,550 per share. One lot equals 100 shares, so he spends Rp 855,000 plus fees. He now owns a very small piece of Bank Central Asia. If the bank earns more profit and investors believe in its future, more people may want to buy the stock and the price may rise. If the bank has problems, the price may fall.', 'Understanding stocks as ownership, not just numbers on a screen, helps you research companies instead of chasing price movements.', 'Buying a stock because the price is going up, without knowing what the company actually does.', 'Explain stock ownership using a familiar Indonesian company like BBCA. Describe how supply and demand move prices, and warn that stock ownership is not guaranteed profit.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'idx-basics-101', 'IDX Rules and Lot Sizes', 'Aturan IDX dan Ukuran Lot', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'idx_basics'), 26, 'intermediate', 65, 6, 'IDX has clear rules: stocks trade in lots of 100 shares, and investor protection programs exist for listed securities.', 'IDX stands for Bursa Efek Indonesia, the Indonesia Stock Exchange. It is the regulated marketplace where stocks and other securities trade. On IDX, stocks are bought and sold in lots, and one standard lot is 100 shares. This means you cannot buy just one share of a listed company; you must buy at least one lot.

IDX trading follows a schedule on business days, usually Monday to Friday. There are also investor protection mechanisms, such as SIPF, the Securities Investor Protection Fund, which provides protection for investors in listed securities within certain limits. These rules exist to make trading fair and to protect small investors. Knowing the rules before you trade prevents rejected orders and confusion. Before you can trade on IDX, you need a securities account with a broker that is registered with OJK. The broker acts as your gateway to the exchange. Always check that your broker is licensed, because unlicensed brokers are not protected by SIPF or OJK supervision.', 'Doni wants to buy shares of TLKM, Telkom Indonesia. He has Rp 1,000,000 and the stock price is Rp 3,800 per share. He thinks he can buy about 263 shares, but on IDX he must buy in lots of 100. He can buy either 100 shares for Rp 380,000 or 200 shares for Rp 760,000. He chooses one lot of 100 shares and keeps the rest in his tabungan.', 'Knowing IDX rules before trading prevents rejected orders and confusion about how much money you actually need to buy a stock.', 'Trying to buy fewer than 100 shares of an IDX stock and not understanding why the order is rejected.', 'Explain IDX lot size and trading days using TLKM or another familiar Indonesian stock. Mention SIPF as an investor protection mechanism without overstating coverage.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'reading-a-stock-page', 'Reading a Stock Page', 'Membaca Halaman Saham', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'stock_analysis'), 27, 'intermediate', 65, 6, 'A stock summary shows the price range, closing price, trading volume, and total company value.', 'A stock page shows numbers that describe how a stock traded during the day. Open is the price at the start of trading. High is the highest price reached. Low is the lowest price reached. Close is the last price before the market closed. Volume is how many shares changed hands. Market capitalization, or market cap, is the total value of all the company''s shares multiplied by the current price.

These numbers describe activity, not quality. A high volume means many people traded that day. A high price does not automatically mean a good company. Reading a stock page is a skill, not a prediction tool. Always look at the full picture and use multiple sources before making any decision. One number never tells the whole story. Market cap helps you understand the size of a company, not whether it is cheap or expensive. A Rp 1,000 stock can be overpriced, and a Rp 10,000 stock can be fairly priced. Price alone does not tell you value. Combine stock page data with company information before forming any opinion.', 'Bayu looks at a simplified BBCA stock card from IDX. It shows open Rp 8,450, high Rp 8,620, low Rp 8,400, close Rp 8,550, and volume 45 million shares. Bayu sees that the price moved between Rp 8,400 and Rp 8,620 during the day and ended at Rp 8,550. The volume of 45 million tells him many people traded BBCA shares that day, but it does not tell him whether the price will rise tomorrow.', 'Reading a stock page helps you move from guessing to observing. It is a basic skill before you do any research or simulation.', 'Thinking that a stock with a big price jump or high volume is automatically a good buy.', 'Walk through open, high, low, close, volume, and market cap using a simplified BBCA stock card. Emphasize that these numbers describe past activity, not future guarantees.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'portfolio-thinking', 'Portfolio Thinking', 'Berpikir Portofolio', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'portfolio'), 28, 'intermediate', 70, 6, 'A portfolio is the whole combination of your holdings, and its mix should match your goals.', 'A portfolio is the combination of all the financial assets you own, such as cash, savings, reksa dana, and stocks. Portfolio thinking means looking at the whole picture instead of judging each item alone. A single stock may be risky, but a small amount of stock inside a larger portfolio can be reasonable.

The right mix depends on your goals and time horizon. Money you need soon should be in safer, easy-to-access places. Money you will not need for years can include more growth assets. As your goals change, your portfolio mix should be reviewed. This is not about picking winners; it is about building a plan that fits your life and keeps you calm during market swings. Rebalancing means adjusting your mix back to your target over time. For example, if stocks have done well and now make up a bigger portion of your portfolio than planned, you might sell some and move the money to safer assets. This keeps your risk level aligned with your original plan.', 'Rina has Rp 10,000,000 in total. She keeps Rp 3,000,000 in a bank tabungan for emergencies, Rp 4,000,000 in a money market reksa dana for a laptop she wants in one year, and Rp 3,000,000 in one stock she likes. This is her portfolio. The missing piece is diversification in the stock part. If her goal were building a pension fund or starting a business in 20–30 years, she might keep less in cash and spread the stock money across more companies or a reksa dana saham.', 'Thinking in portfolios helps you avoid the trap of putting everything into one trendy stock and then panicking when it falls.', 'Judging your financial health by one stock while ignoring cash, debt, and other holdings.', 'Explain portfolio as the total set of holdings. Ask the user about their goal and time horizon, then discuss how the mix of cash, reksa dana, and stocks might change.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'taxes-on-returns', 'Taxes on Your Returns', 'Pajak atas Imbal Hasil', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'taxes'), 29, 'advanced', 65, 6, 'Investment gains may be taxed, so the return you keep can be lower than the headline number.', 'When you earn money from investments, the government may take a portion as tax. In Indonesia, different types of return can be taxed differently. For example, interest from deposits and dividends from stocks may both be subject to tax. The amount you actually keep after tax is called your net return, while the amount before tax is called your gross return.

Tax rules can change, and the details depend on your situation and the type of account. The key lesson is to compare investments using net return, not just the advertised gross return. Paying tax is a legal obligation, but you can structure your finances sensibly within the rules. For specific advice, consult a tax professional or official guidance from the tax office. Some long-term savings may have tax incentives under Indonesian rules, but the rules change. Always check current regulations from the tax office (Direktorat Jenderal Pajak) or a qualified advisor. The main point is to see investing as a net-return game, where what you keep matters more than what you earn on paper.', 'Doni earns Rp 500,000 in interest from a deposito and Rp 1,000,000 from a stock dividend during the year. His bank statement shows the gross amounts, but after tax he keeps less. When Doni compares two investments, he should look at the net amount he will actually receive, not just the headline rate advertised by the product.', 'Ignoring taxes can make an investment look better than it really is. Net return is what matters for your actual wealth.', 'Comparing investments only by their advertised gross return without considering tax and fees.', 'Explain gross return, net return, and the idea that different returns may be taxed. Use a deposito interest and dividend example. Advise consulting a tax professional for personal situations.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'macro-indicators', 'Macro Indicators to Watch', 'Indikator Makro yang Perlu Diperhatikan', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'macro_indicators'), 30, 'advanced', 65, 6, 'Big-picture signals like BI Rate, inflation, and exchange rates affect prices, interest, and your purchasing power.', 'Macro indicators are large-scale signals that describe the health of the economy. Three important ones in Indonesia are the BI Rate, which is the central bank''s benchmark interest rate; inflation, which measures how fast prices rise; and the exchange rate, which shows how much rupiah is needed to buy one US dollar (dolar AS) or other currency.

These indicators affect your money indirectly. When BI raises the BI Rate, bank deposit interest may rise, but borrowing becomes more expensive and stock prices may become more volatile. When the rupiah weakens against the US dollar, imported gadgets and fuel can become more expensive. You cannot control these indicators, but knowing they exist helps you understand why prices and investment values move without reacting to every headline. You do not need to predict these indicators perfectly. Even professional economists disagree. What matters is recognizing their general direction so you are not shocked when deposit rates rise, loan installments increase, or import prices change. Stay informed, but stay focused on your own plan.', 'When Bank Indonesia raises the BI Rate, the interest on Doni''s Rp 10,000,000 deposito might increase from 3% to 4%. At the same time, his friend who wants to buy a new laptop imported from abroad for Rp 12,000,000 notices the price goes up because the rupiah weakened against the US dollar (dolar AS). Doni benefits slightly from higher deposit interest, but his friend pays more for the same gadget.', 'Macro indicators explain the background noise of prices and investment returns. Understanding them helps you focus on what you can control instead of panicking over news.', 'Trying to time investments perfectly based on macro news, which even experts usually fail to do.', 'Define BI Rate, inflation, and exchange rate using Indonesian examples. Explain how each can affect savings interest, loan costs, and import prices. Warn against trying to time markets based on macro news.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'behavioral-bias-intro', 'Behavioral Bias Intro', 'Pengenalan Bias Perilaku', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'behavioral_finance'), 31, 'advanced', 65, 6, 'Your emotions can push you to follow the crowd, fear losses, or chase trends without thinking.', 'Behavioral bias means mental shortcuts that can lead to poor financial decisions. Three common biases are FOMO, or fear of missing out, which makes you buy something just because others are buying it; loss aversion, which makes the pain of losing money feel stronger than the joy of gaining the same amount; and herd behavior, which makes you follow the crowd without doing your own research.

These biases are normal, but they can be costly. The best defense is to slow down, write down your reason for a decision, and check it against your goals. If you are buying because of a viral trend, that is a signal to pause. Good investing is usually boring, while bad investing is often exciting. Awareness does not remove emotion, but it gives you a chance to decide with your head as well as your feelings. One practical technique is to set investment rules in advance, such as only buying after you have read the company''s annual report or only investing a fixed amount each month. Rules reduce the number of emotional decisions you have to make in the moment, which is when biases are strongest.', 'All of Bayu''s friends are buying GOTO stock with Rp 1,000,000 each because of a viral TikTok video saying the price will double. Bayu feels FOMO because he does not want to miss out. He also feels loss aversion because he is afraid of losing his Rp 1,000,000. Instead of buying immediately, he writes down his reason: ''Because TikTok said so.'' He realizes this is herd behavior, not research. He decides to learn more about the company first and only invests an amount he can afford to lose.', 'Recognizing your own biases is one of the most valuable investing skills. It protects you from hype, panic, and regret.', 'Buying an investment because it feels urgent or popular, without checking whether it fits your plan.', 'Explain FOMO, loss aversion, and herd behavior using a TikTok stock trend example. Encourage the user to slow down, write their reason, and check against their goals.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE),
  (gen_random_uuid(), 'building-financial-plan', 'Building Your Financial Plan', 'Membangun Rencana Keuanganmu', (SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'financial_planning'), 32, 'advanced', 70, 6, 'A financial plan puts goals, emergency savings, debt, protection, and investing into one clear order.', 'A financial plan is a simple map of where your money should go. For most young Indonesians, the first steps are: cover basic needs, build a small emergency fund, pay off high-interest debt, protect yourself with health coverage such as BPJS Kesehatan or private health insurance if possible, and only then consider investing. The order matters because there is no point chasing investment returns while an emergency or high-interest debt can wipe you out.

Your plan should match your life. If you have no debt and family support, you may start investing sooner. If you have unstable income, build a bigger emergency fund first. Review your plan every few months as your income, goals, and responsibilities change. A plan is not a one-time document; it is a habit of checking where you are and what comes next. Protection includes health coverage such as BPJS Kesehatan and, later in life, life insurance if you have dependents. At 18, the most important protection is usually health coverage, because one hospital bill can wipe out savings. Once these foundations are solid, investing becomes a way to grow money rather than a gamble.', 'Ani is 19 and earns Rp 2,500,000 per month from freelance design. She has no emergency fund and Rp 2,000,000 in PayLater debt with high interest. Her financial plan starts with covering basic needs, building a one-month emergency fund of Rp 1,500,000, then aggressively paying off the PayLater debt. Only after the debt is gone does she think about investing Rp 100,000 per month in a reksa dana.', 'A plan turns random financial advice into personal action. It helps you know what to do next, no matter how small your income is.', 'Jumping straight into investing before building an emergency fund or paying off high-interest debt.', 'Help the user order the five basic steps of a financial plan: needs, emergency fund, high-interest debt, protection, and investing. Use a young freelancer example and emphasize that the order depends on personal circumstances.', 'approved', 'Koin Content Reviewer', NOW(), TRUE)
